    "body": "agent-note:v1\nstate: picked-up → blocked (escalated to Drew — physical access required)\nqueue: repo-health-code-pilot\ntask: 7624 (blocked, reason class `escalated-to-user`)\nby: openchamber-scheduled-lane\nat: 2026-11-03T14:00Z\n\n## Verdict: still live, and not workable from this repo — the blocker is hardware time\n\nRevalidated at current `main` (`4c75cc808`):\n\n- `planning/roadmaps/basement-cluster.md:13` still carries this epic as a **planned** row — *\"Gather hardware stats for all ~12 machines and pick the first 3–5 cluster nodes\"* — with no status change since the roadmap was written.\n- No inventory records exist anywhere in the repo: `planning/roadmaps/` holds only the roadmap, the electrician brief and the repo roadmaps; a `Threadripper` search across tracked Markdown returns the roadmap and the electrician brief, no per-machine records. So the epic's acceptance (one committed record per machine and drive, a named ordered first-node list) is **not** quietly done.\n- Nothing else in the queue or in open PRs covers it; this is not a duplicate and there is no superseding artifact.\n\n## Why this is Drew's call, not a worker's\n\nThe epic's own execution mode is `hybrid`, and the missing half is physical: *\"gathering stats needs machines powered and reachable (physical), or BMC/KVM access once cabled.\"* A code-queue worker cannot power twelve machines, cable BMC, or read SMART data off drives that are still in the box (the 8 HDDs the epic explicitly says have no baseline). Everything a worker could contribute — the record schema, the analysis templates — is already described as online, and making those up front without a single real record would be busywork against a format nobody has validated.\n\nSo the exact decision required:\n\n1. **A hardware window** where the ~12 machines are powered and reachable (or BMC/KVM cabled), so the inventory can actually be collected.\n2. **Who collects it** — Drew on site, or a session driven against reachable hosts once they are up.\n\n## Handling\n\n- Queue task blocked (`escalated-to-user`), issue labelled `user/escalated` in place of `status/needs-triage`; `queue/blocked` + `status/blocked` set, `work/assigned` kept while it waits.\n- When the window is scheduled, the task resumes with this note as the brief: collect per-machine records (CPU, board, RAM, every NVMe/HDD with model + SMART, NICs, GPUs), commit them under `planning/roadmaps/` (or wherever #1080 names), then produce the ranked first-node list.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
